## ⚡ Quick Start (5 Minutes)

If you're familiar with Python, here's the fastest way to get started:

```bash
# Copy and paste everything below
git clone https://github.com/yourusername/voice-cloning-project.git
cd voice-cloning-project
python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate  # On Windows: venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
python gradio_ui.py


📥 Step-by-Step Installation
Step 1: Install Python
Windows:

Go to python.org

Download Python 3.10

Run the installer

IMPORTANT: Check "Add Python to PATH" during installation

macOS:

bash
# Using Homebrew
brew install python@3.10

# Or download from python.org
Linux (Ubuntu/Debian):

bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install python3.10 python3.10-venv python3-pip
Verify installation:

bash
python --version
You should see: Python 3.10.x

Step 2: Install Git
Windows: Download from git-scm.com

macOS:

bash
# Using Homebrew
brew install git

# Or use built-in git (may need to install Xcode Command Line Tools)
Linux:

bash
sudo apt install git  # Ubuntu/Debian
Verify installation:

bash
git --version
Step 3: Clone the Repository
Open a terminal/command prompt and run:

bash
git clone https://github.com/yourusername/voice-cloning-project.git
cd voice-cloning-project
Step 4: Create Virtual Environment
Windows (Command Prompt):

bash
python -m venv venv
venv\Scripts\activate
Windows (PowerShell):

powershell
python -m venv venv
.\venv\Scripts\Activate
macOS/Linux:

bash
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
When successful, you'll see (venv) at the beginning of your terminal prompt.

Step 5: Install Requirements
First, upgrade pip:

bash
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
Then install all dependencies:

bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
This will take 5-10 minutes depending on your internet speed. The system will download:

PyTorch (deep learning framework)

XTTS-v2 model (1.8 GB)

Various audio processing libraries

Step 6: Verify Installation
Run this test to ensure everything is working:

bash
python -c "import torch; print(f'PyTorch: {torch.__version__}'); print(f'CUDA available: {torch.cuda.is_available()}')"
You should see:

text
PyTorch: 2.5.1
CUDA available: True  # (or False if no GPU)
🎤 Adding Your Voices
Step 1: Prepare Your Voice Files
Audio Requirements:

Format: WAV (recommended) or MP3

Duration: 15-30 seconds (optimal for best quality)

Quality: Clear speech, minimal background noise

Content: Natural speech (not singing)

Step 2: Create Speaker Folders
Create folders for each speaker you want to add:

bash
# Create folders for 4 speakers (you can add more later)
mkdir -p voices/speaker1 voices/speaker2 voices/speaker3 voices/speaker4
Step 3: Add Your Audio Files
Place your audio files in the folders and rename them to reference.wav:

text
voice-cloning-project/
├── voices/
│   ├── speaker1/
│   │   └── reference.wav    # Your first voice
│   ├── speaker2/
│   │   └── reference.wav    # Your second voice
│   ├── speaker3/
│   │   └── reference.wav    # Your third voice
│   └── speaker4/
│       └── reference.wav    # Your fourth voice
Step 4: Register Your Voices
Run the registration script:

bash
python scripts/register_voices.py
Follow the interactive prompts:

The script will find your audio files automatically

Type y to confirm each voice

Wait for registration to complete

You'll see output like:

text
--- Registering: speaker1 ---
Found existing file: voices\speaker1\reference.wav
Use this file for speaker1? (y/n): y
✓ speaker1 registered: Voice added: 25.3s
